BRAIN UNIT — POWER + DISPLAY
1. Power path (battery → ESP32-S3)
Battery → TP4056 charger → switch → MT3608 boost (5.00V) → ESP32-S3 "5V" pin

Why every part is needed:

Charger board – charges the cell and protects it from over-discharge/short. Required.
Switch – turns the whole unit off, and lets you charge with the unit off. Required.
Boost converter – a LiPo is 3.0–4.2V. The board's 5V pin needs a real 5V. Required.
Capacitor – smooths the boost output so the ESP32 doesn't reset during BLE/display bursts. Strongly recommended.

Note on 700mAh: ESP32-S3 + display will pull roughly 0.5–0.7A from the battery at peaks. That's near 1C for a 700mAh cell. It will work, but expect ~1–1.5 hours runtime and check the battery for warmth in the first test.

2. Power wiring — exact connections

Before soldering anything: meter on the battery leads. Whichever lead reads positive is +. Ignore wire color.

From	To
Battery +	Charger B+
Battery –	Charger B–
Charger OUT+	Switch middle pin
Switch right pin	Boost VIN+
Charger OUT–	Boost VIN–
Switch left pin	nothing — heat-shrink it

STOP HERE. Switch on. Meter on VOUT+ / VOUT–. Turn the blue screw until it reads 5.00V. (Many turns — it's slow. If it doesn't move, keep turning; the trimmer often needs 10+ turns before anything changes.) Switch off, then on again, confirm it's still 5.00V.

Then continue:

From	To
Boost VOUT+	ESP32-S3 pin marked 5V
Boost VOUT–	ESP32-S3 pin marked GND
Capacitor + (long leg / no stripe)	Boost VOUT+
Capacitor – (stripe side)	Boost VOUT–

Capacitor: 220–470µF, rated 10V or more. Solder it right at the boost output pads. If you have a 0.1µF ceramic, put it across the same two pads (no polarity).

Hard rule: When the switch is ON, do not plug USB into the ESP32. Two 5V sources fighting can damage the board. USB only when the switch is OFF.

3. Display wiring (LCD → ESP32-S3)
Display pin	ESP32-S3 pin
GND	GND
VCC	3V3 (see section 4)
SCK	GPIO 12
SDA	GPIO 11
RST	GPIO 10
DC	GPIO 9
CS	GPIO 8

Wires under 10cm. Never connect or disconnect the display while the board is powered.

4. LCD VCC: 3.3V or 5V?

Use 3.3V. Here's the logic:

The display controller chip itself is a 3.3V part. 5V straight to it kills it.
Some modules have a small 3-pin regulator on the back so they can take 5V. If yours has one, 3.3V still works fine (regulator just passes it through slightly lower).
So 3.3V is the only choice that cannot destroy the display no matter which version you have.

What to check first (30 seconds): Flip the display over. Look near the VCC pin for a tiny 3-pin chip marked 662K, 4A2, or similar.

Found one → 3.3V works, 5V would also work. Use 3.3V anyway.
No chip → 3.3V is the only safe option.

Either way: VCC → 3V3. The board's 3.3V regulator has plenty of margin for the ~60–80mA the screen draws.

5. Are GPIO 8–12 safe on this board?

Yes. Checked against the ESP32-S3 datasheet:

Boot/strapping pins are 0, 3, 45, 46 — not used here.
USB is 19, 20 — not used.
Flash is 26–32 — not used, not broken out.
PSRAM (the R8 part) is 33–37 — not used.
UART0 is 43, 44 — not used.
Onboard RGB LED is 48 (sometimes 38) — not used.
GPIO 10, 11, 12 are actually the chip's native SPI pins — best possible choice for a display.

Nothing to worry about. Do not change these to anything in the ranges above.

6. Test before permanent wiring

A. Power chain, no ESP32 attached

Boost reads 5.00V unloaded ✔
Switch OFF → VIN+ reads 0V ✔
Charger: plug USB-C into the charger board with the switch OFF. Red/blue LED lights, board barely warm. ✔
Meter between the wire going to the "5V" pin and the wire going to "GND": reads +5.00V (positive, not negative). ✔

B. Display on USB (no battery yet)
Wire display per section 3. Plug ESP32 USB. Run the test sketch below.

C. Battery last
Unplug USB. Connect boost output. Switch on. Board boots, display works.

7. Step-by-step order
Step 1 — ESP32 alone, USB

Plug in the UART USB port. Upload a blank sketch with Serial.println("alive"). Confirm serial output. Done.

Step 2 — Display, still USB

Wire the 7 display wires. Install TFT_eSPI library. In its User_Setup.h, comment out everything else and set:

#define ST7789_DRIVER        // try this first
#define TFT_WIDTH  240
#define TFT_HEIGHT 320
#define TFT_MOSI 11
#define TFT_SCLK 12
#define TFT_CS    8
#define TFT_DC    9
#define TFT_RST  10
#define TFT_MISO -1
#define LOAD_GLCD
#define SPI_FREQUENCY 27000000

Test sketch:

#include <TFT_eSPI.h>
TFT_eSPI tft;
void setup() {
  tft.init(); tft.setRotation(0);
  tft.fillScreen(TFT_RED);   delay(500);
  tft.fillScreen(TFT_GREEN); delay(500);
  tft.fillScreen(TFT_BLUE);  delay(500);
  tft.fillScreen(TFT_BLACK);
  tft.setTextColor(TFT_WHITE); tft.setTextSize(3);
  tft.drawString("OK", 80, 140);
}
void loop() {}
Red, green, blue, "OK" → driver correct. Done.
Colors wrong/inverted → add #define TFT_INVERSION_ON or TFT_RGB_ORDER TFT_BGR.
Garbage/blank → change ST7789_DRIVER to ILI9341_DRIVER and retry. Wrong driver can't damage anything, only shows junk.
Still blank → backlight on but no image = wiring order; check SCK/SDA aren't swapped.
Step 3 — Battery power
Unplug USB.
Connect boost VOUT+ → 5V, VOUT– → GND (with capacitor already on).
Switch ON. Display shows the test.
Meter across the 5V/GND pins while it runs: should stay 4.8–5.1V.
Run 15 minutes. Touch the boost inductor, the battery, the ESP32 regulator. Warm is fine. Too hot to hold = stop.
Switch OFF, charge via charger USB-C, confirm the charge LED changes when full.

Only after this passes do you put anything in the tin.

Don't
Don't plug ESP32 USB in while the switch is on.
Don't put 5V on VCC — 3V3 only.
Don't touch the blue screw once the ESP32 is connected.
Don't connect/disconnect the display while powered.
Don't let any board underside touch the tin.
